Subject: [PATCH] feat(approval): provenance review for smart approval

With approvals.smart_provenance (false = upstream) and mode smart, every tool call that is not plainly read-only is reviewed in two stages, in the shape of a coding agent's auto-mode classifier. Stage 1 (approvals.smart_fast_model) is a one-token block check that errs toward blocking; only its blocks reach stage 2 (approvals.smart_model), a reasoning check that applies allow exceptions and user intent and names the rule it blocks on. The review sees the user's own messages (the 0068 classifier: never subagent results, peer-agent messages or recalled memory), the session's earlier non-read-only tool calls with an ok/error/blocked outcome line, and repo instruction files labeled as not the user. It never sees tool results, so text in a file, page, command output or another agent's message cannot authorize a risky action. Rules: one hard tier (sensitive data leaving the trust boundary) that no chat words clear, soft rules that clear when the user's words name the action and its target (or the target is clear from what the user or the agent's own work established), and allow exceptions; approvals.smart_rules extends them. A hard block is final; a soft block or an unreadable review goes to a one-time owner override card reading 'Smart approval rule "<name>": <reason>', and is blocked when nobody can answer. Plain project file writes are no longer judged by their text; writes to instruction, config, credential and shell-startup files are reviewed. Subagents are judged against the root chat's user messages.
