+### Provenance review
+
+With `approvals.smart_provenance: true` (and `mode: smart`), smart approval reviews every tool call that is not plainly read-only, not only commands the pattern list flags. The reviewer sees the user's own messages, the session's earlier non-read-only tool calls with an `ok`/`error`/`blocked` outcome line each, and the project's instruction files labeled as repository content. It never sees tool results, so text in a file, web page, command output or another agent's message cannot speak for the user. A risky action runs only when the user's own words name the action and its target (or the target is clear from what the user or the agent's own work established), or an allow exception covers it. Exfiltration of sensitive data to an outside destination is a hard rule that nothing said in chat clears. Writes inside the working directory skip the review unless they touch instruction, config, credential or shell-startup files.
+
+The review has two stages: a one-token check that errs toward blocking (`smart_fast_model`), and, only for its blocks, a reasoning check that applies exceptions and user intent (`smart_model`). A block names its rule, for example `Smart approval rule "Git Destructive": force push to main would drop a teammate's commit`; the agent gets that text and, when someone can answer, the approval card offers a one-time override. A reviewer reply that cannot be read never allows the action.
+
+```yaml
+approvals:
+  mode: smart
+  smart_provenance: true
+  smart_fast_model: ""     # empty = auxiliary.approval; or {provider, model, base_url}
+  smart_model: ""
+  smart_rules:
+    environment:
+      - "**Trusted internal domains**: git.example.com"
+    soft_block:
+      - {name: "Database Migrations", must_name: "the database", text: "Running schema migrations."}
+```
